# Laravel API origin, no trailing slash. The storefront authenticates with
# Sanctum session cookies (credentials: include), so this host must be listed
# in the API's SANCTUM_STATEFUL_DOMAINS and CORS allowed origins. Left EMPTY
# in production, where nginx serves the app and the API from one origin and a
# same-origin /api/* request needs no host at all.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Google Maps JS + Places, for the map view of "Near you" on /discover.
#
# Optional, and honestly so: with no key here the List/Map choice never
# renders and the page is the list it has always been. The map is a second
# way to read the same stores, never the only way to find them, so an
# environment without a key is a supported one rather than a broken one.
#
# Like every NEXT_PUBLIC_* value this is inlined into the browser bundle at
# build time and is therefore public by definition — restrict it by HTTP
# referrer in the Google console instead of trying to keep it secret, and
# remember to add the storefront's own host to that list or the map fails
# silently with nothing but a console warning to say why.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# Base path when the app is served from a sub-path rather than the domain
# root; next.config.mjs feeds it to both basePath and assetPrefix. Unset for
# a normal deployment.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH=

# --- server-only (never reaches the browser) ---------------------------------

# Absolute API origin for server-rendered fetches, when it differs from the
# public one — a direct origin address that bypasses the CDN edge, say. Unset
# falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL and then to the canonical API host.
# API_INTERNAL_URL=

# Shared secret that exempts this server's SSR fetches from the API's per-IP
# discovery throttle; must match the API's services.discovery.internal_token.
# Every SSR request leaves one IP, so without it a burst of store-page
# renders would 429 each other. Unset, behaviour is unchanged.
# DISCOVERY_INTERNAL_TOKEN=
